arm64: make cooperative kernel busy waits advance virtual time

The Harmony clock advances only at guest exits. A kernel busy wait that uses
cpu_relax() can therefore deadlock: its sched_clock() deadline remains frozen,
need_resched() never becomes true, and the task that could release the resource
never runs. Reader-owned rwsem optimistic spinning is one concrete trigger.

Make every kernel cpu_relax() iteration ring the existing deterministic
virtual-time MMIO tick before issuing YIELD. The tick is derived solely from a
guest execution boundary and uses no machine counter or host time. Keep the
vDSO definition unchanged: BUILD_VDSO code runs in userspace and cannot access
the kernel's MMIO mapping.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a diff under the Harmony
repository's designated guest-kernel patch exception.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/include/asm/vdso/processor.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/include/asm/vdso/processor.h
@@ -6,9 +6,19 @@
 
 #ifndef __ASSEMBLY__
 
+#if defined(CONFIG_HARMONY_ARM_PVCLOCK) && !defined(BUILD_VDSO)
+void harmony_arm_virtual_time_tick(void);
+
+static inline void cpu_relax(void)
+{
+	harmony_arm_virtual_time_tick();
+	asm volatile("yield" ::: "memory");
+}
+#else
 static inline void cpu_relax(void)
 {
 	asm volatile("yield" ::: "memory");
 }
+#endif
 
 #endif /* __ASSEMBLY__ */
 
